{
  "abstract": "The Biane–Pitman–Yor theta law is a binary smoothing fixed point whose moment-generating function has a double pole at $\\pi^2$. We determine the nonlinear boundary layer formed by entire approximants from a complete logarithmically compressed theta source. For every fixed positive heat, exact reciprocal-radius matching gives strict domination of all integer moments and a bounded monotone cotangent profile. The source comparison uses a 160-bit interval certificate for the complete kernel, with every omitted theta mode and the unbounded spatial tail included. At depth $n$, the profile has scale $\\epsilon_n=(7/12)^{7n/17}$ and converges to $\\mathbb E\\exp(-D z^{17/7}W)$, where $W$ is the classical mean-one binary cascade with edge weights $(1-C)^{17/7}/(7/12)$. A fractional positive eigenfunction controls the exact state-dependent backward tree in $L^{3/2}$, including its negative terminal states; a second-moment argument would fail. Coefficient domination upgrades the real limit to locally uniform holomorphic convergence on the interior half-plane of the scaled pole. An explicit exponential tilt of the actual branching laws then converges to a finite positive boundary measure, whose Mellin transform is expressed through negative cascade moments with its Gamma factor retained. The source class includes independently real-zero critical Mellin seeds. The result does not establish zero preservation, convergence beyond the pole, unweighted tilted-mass convergence, or the Riemann hypothesis.",
